      "data": {
        "value": "<p><strong>TEST INFORMATION:</strong></p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sample Type</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Sample Quality &amp; Quantity</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Method</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Clinical Indication</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>EDTA Peripheral Blood</td>\n<td>Good &amp; Sufficient</td>\n<td>RT-PCR</td>\n<td>\n<p>CML&nbsp;</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>RESULT:</strong></p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellspacing=\"1\" cellpadding=\"1\" align=\"center\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ABL1 copies</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BCR-ABL1 copies</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BCR-ABL1/ABL1 (%)</strong></td>\n<td><strong>BCR-ABL1/ABL1 (IS%)</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Transcript Detected</strong></td>\n<td><strong>MMR Achieved</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>23409.0</p>\n</td>\n<td>13.0</td>\n<td>\n<p>0.055%</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>0.038</strong><strong>%</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td><strong>p210 (Major)</strong></td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>YES</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>INTERPRETATION:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The ratio of BCR-ABL1/ABL1 is 0</strong><strong>.038%</strong><strong> (IS-scale)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Please correlate result with complete hemogram, bone marrow analysis, clinical findings, and previous BCR-ABL1&nbsp;report (if any).</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<img src=\"https://radiology-flabs.s3.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/2c15a696236a92a72406.png\" data-cke-saved-src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"></p>\n<p><em><strong>Note: BCR-ABL1 p210 (IS) scores: </strong>This<strong>&nbsp;</strong>score uses a standard baseline of 100%. Changes in (IS) scores are often described in terms of &ldquo;log changes.&rdquo; Log changes can decrease or increase. A log increase means that the value has gone up at least 10 times. <strong>A log increase while being treated is cause for concern</strong>.</em>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>&nbsp;Response Type</strong></td>\n<td><strong>&nbsp;Interpretation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&nbsp;Complete Hematologic Response (CHR)</td>\n<td>&nbsp;Blood counts are normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&nbsp;Complete Cytogenetic Response (CCyR)</td>\n<td>&nbsp;No Philadelphia chromosomes (Ph-)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&nbsp;Major cytogenetic response (MCyR)</td>\n<td>&nbsp;Ph+ are between 0% and 35%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&nbsp;Major Molecular Response (MMR)</td>\n<td>&nbsp;BCR-ABL1 (IS) is 0.1% or less (MR3;&nbsp;3-Log reduction)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&nbsp;Deep Molecular Response (DMR)</td>\n<td>&nbsp;BCR-ABL1 (IS) is 0.01% or less (MR4.0; 4-Log reduction)<br>&nbsp;BCR-ABL1 (IS) is 0.0032% or less (MR4.5; 4.5-Log reduction)<br>&nbsp;BCR-ABL1 (IS) is 0.001% or less (MR5; 5-Log reduction)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"cke_pagebreak\" title=\"Page Break\" contenteditable=\"false\" aria-label=\"Page Break\" data-cke-display-name=\"pagebreak\" data-cke-pagebreak=\"1\">&nbsp;</div>\n<p><strong>CLINICAL UTILITY</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>This test quantifies BCR-ABL1 transcripts (major-p210, minor-p190&nbsp;and micro-p230)&nbsp;for diagnostic and ongoing therapeutic monitoring.&nbsp;</li>\n<li>BCR-ABL1 transcript levels are expressed as a percent ratio of BCR-ABL1 to the normalizing ABL1 transcript. For the p210 transcript, quantitation in further adjusted to the international scale (IS).</li>\n<li>Optimal therapy in CML is associated with the transcript levels below the major molecular response (MMR) milestone indicates by a BCR-ABL1 (IS) below 0.1%.</li>\n<li>BCR-ABL1 fusions are reported in more than 90% of CML&nbsp;cases, 25-35% of adult acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) cases, and 3-5% of childhood ALL cases.</li>\n<li>Several targeted TKIs are approved by the FDA for activated BCR-ABL1, primarily in hematological cancers. These include imatinib (2001), dasatinib&nbsp;(2006), and ponatinib&nbsp;(2012) in CML and ALL, as well as nilotinib (2007) and bosutinib (2012) in CML.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>MONITORING DISEASE RESPONSE TO TYROSINE KINASE INHIBITOR (TKIS) THERAPY IN CML:</strong></p>\n<p>To see how well CML is responding to targeted therapy, the patient is monitored with this test using IS. This test is very sensitive to detect very low levels of BCR- ABL1. The patient will have a BCR-ABL1 p210 (IS) at diagnosis and every 3 months once the patient start treatment. After BCR-ABL1 p210 (IS) is 1% or less, this test is recommended every 3 months for 2 years, then every 3 to 6 months. It is very important to keep up with testing and doctor visits.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellspacing=\"1\" cellpadding=\"1\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"4\"><strong>Treatment Milestones using BCR-ABL1 (IS)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&nbsp;</td>\n<td><strong>at 3 months&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>at 6 months</strong></td>\n<td><strong>at 12 months</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>BCR-ABL1 (IS) is more than 10%</td>\n<td><strong>Possible TKI Resistance</strong></td>\n<td><strong>TKI Resistance</strong></td>\n<td><strong>TKI Resistance</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>BCR-ABL1 (IS) is between 10% and 1%</td>\n<td>Milestone met</td>\n<td>Milestone met</td>\n<td><strong>Possible TKI Resistance</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>BCR-ABL1 (IS) is between 1% and 0.1%</td>\n<td>Milestone met</td>\n<td>Milestone met</td>\n<td>-Milestone met if goal is long term survival<br>-Milestone not met if goal is treatment-free remission</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>BCR-ABL1 (IS) is 0.1% or less</td>\n<td>Milestone met</td>\n<td>Milestone met</td>\n<td>Milestone met</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong><em>(Adopted from NCCN guidelines for patient, Chronic Myeloid Leukemia-2021.)</em></strong><br><br><strong>NOTE:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>This test detects the major (M-BCR), minor (m-BCR) and micro breakpoints corresponding to p210, p190 and p230 protein. Detection of transcripts for others rare translocation are out of scope of this assay.</li>\n<li>Absolute copies of BCR-ABL1 and ABL1 may vary according to the sample quality.&nbsp;</li>\n<li>Minimum amplification of ABL control gene must be 10,000 copies&nbsp;to achive MR3,&nbsp;10000&ndash;31999&nbsp;to achive MR4,&nbsp;32000&ndash;99999&nbsp;to achive MR4.5, &gt;100,000&nbsp;to achive MR5.</li>\n<li>LOD of the assay is 0.001% in the background of &gt;100,000 copies of ABL1.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>REFERENCES:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>H&ouml;glund M et al. Epidemiology of chronic myeloid leukaemia: an&nbsp;update. Ann Hematol. 2015 Apr;94 Suppl 2:S241-7.</li>\n<li>Salesse S et al. BCR/ABL 1: from molecular mechanisms of leukaemia&nbsp;induction to treatment of chronic myelogenous leukaemia, Oncogene, 21 (2002), pp.&nbsp;8547&ndash;8559.</li>\n<li>Faderl S et al. The biology of&nbsp;chronic myeloid leukemia. New England Journal of Medicine 1999;341(3):164-72.</li>\n<li>Faderl S et al. Chronic myelogenous leukemia: Biology and therapy. Annals of Internal Medicine 1999 Aug 3;131(3):207-19.</li>\n<li>Gils FJ et al.&nbsp;Molecular Monitoring of BCR-ABL1 Transcripts&mdash;Standardization Needed to Properly Use, and Further Investigate the Value of, a Critical Surrogate Marker for Success in Therapy of Chronic Myeloid Leukemia. US Oncology &amp; Hematology, 2011;7(2):138-42.</li>\n<li>Cross NCP&nbsp;et al. Standardized definitions of molecular response in chronic myeloid leukemia. Leukemia 2012;26:2172&ndash;5.</li>\n<li>Achkar et al. A rare chronic myeloid leukemia case with Philadelphia chromosome, BCR-ABL e13a3 transcript and complex translocation involving four different chromosomes. Oncol Lett. 2010 Sep;1(5):797-800.</li>\n<li>De et al. Cytogenetics in pre-B and B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia: a study of 208 patients diagnosed between 1981 and 2008. Cancer Genet. Cytogenet. 2010 Jul 1;200(1):8-15.</li>\n<li>Leoni et al. Tyrosine kinase inhibitors in BCR-ABL positive acute lymphoblastic leukemia. Haematologica. 2015 Mar;100(3):295-9.</li>\n</ul>",
